      "id": "https://www.archdaily.com/1186463/flat-land-hidden-water-architecture-on-reclaimed-ground",
      "url": "https://www.archdaily.com/1186463/flat-land-hidden-water-architecture-on-reclaimed-ground",
      "title": "[ArchDaily] Flat Land, Hidden Water: Architecture on Reclaimed Ground",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://images.adsttc.com/media/images/6ac5/cc06/27ac/8d01/89cb/be81/large_jpg/flat-land-hidden-water-architecture-on-reclaimed-ground_1.jpg?1791347725\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\n<p>Reclaimed land is often presented as <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/1035860/the-illusion-of-level-detailing-for-water-in-flat-architecture\">new ground: flat</a>, available, and ready for architecture. It promises cities more space where space is scarce, allowing them to extend <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/1025042/waterfronts-heritage-and-innovation-recent-highlights-from-big-gehl-and-more-in-architecture-now\">waterfronts</a> and build cultural districts. Sometimes, these newly <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/1016218/turning-water-into-land-major-landfill-projects-around-the-world\">claimed lands</a> may also develop into financial centers, create parks, and imagine futures beyond the limits of existing land. Yet reclaimed land is never simply land. It is an engineered surface, produced through fill, seawalls, drainage, and soil stabilization — a long-term negotiation with the water it claims to have displaced.</p>",
      "date_published": "2026-10-09T04:00:00+00:00",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "ArchDaily",
          "url": "https://www.archdaily.com"
        }
      ],
      "image": "https://images.adsttc.com/media/images/6ac5/cc06/27ac/8d01/89cb/be81/large_jpg/flat-land-hidden-water-architecture-on-reclaimed-ground_1.jpg?1791347725",
      "_score": 45,
