    {
      "id": "https://www.archdaily.com/1185414/unearthing-the-ground-architecture-and-the-politics-of-waste",
      "url": "https://www.archdaily.com/1185414/unearthing-the-ground-architecture-and-the-politics-of-waste",
      "title": "[ArchDaily] Unearthing the Ground: Architecture and the Politics of Waste",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://images.adsttc.com/media/images/6aaf/fc15/8d91/7901/8920/59e2/large_jpg/unearthing-the-ground-architecture-and-the-politics-of-waste_15.jpg?1789918235\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\n<p>In 1948, <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/tag/robert-moses\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Robert Moses</a> established the Fresh Kills Landfill on the western shore of <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/tag/staten-island\">Staten Island</a>. The site, chosen for its remote marshland and cheap land value, was meant to be temporary, but by 1955 it was the largest landfill in the world, receiving up to 29,000 tons of refuse per day at its 1991 peak. By 2001, after more than fifty years of continuous operation, Fresh Kills covered 2,200 acres, roughly three times the area of <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/tag/central-park\">Central Park</a>, and was, unspectacularly, visible from space. When it started, Moses didn't expect to design a dump but, without calling it that, a new landform with four artificial hills rising up to sixty meters above sea level built entirely from the accumulated residue of eight million daily lives.</p>",
